  c2:{title:"Intelligence, Evaluation & Domains",intro:"How do we measure intelligence? Which domains apply AI today? Covers Turing Test, evaluation metrics, and real-world applications.",sections:[{heading:"The Turing Test",body:"Turing proposed a behavioural test: if a machine fools a human judge in conversation, it exhibits intelligence. Searle's Chinese Room argues syntax ≠ semantics.",bullets:["Tests behavioural imitation","Does not prove understanding","Chinese Room: syntax ≠ semantics"]},{heading:"Evaluation Metrics",body:"Classification uses accuracy, precision, recall, F1. Regression uses MSE, R². No single metric tells the full story — always check which metric is reported.",bullets:["Accuracy: correct / total","Precision: TP / (TP + FP)","Recall: TP / (TP + FN)","F1: harmonic mean of P and R"]},{heading:"Real-World Domains",body:"AI is deployed in healthcare, finance, transportation, retail, education. Each domain carries unique risk profiles.",bullets:["Healthcare: diagnostics","Finance: fraud detection","Retail: recommendations"]}],tip:"Always check which metric a system reports — high accuracy can mask poor recall."},
  c3:{title:"Responsible AI & Governance",intro:"Building AI that is fair, transparent, and accountable. Covers bias sources, fairness metrics, and governance frameworks.",sections:[{heading:"Sources of Bias",body:"Bias enters through training data, feature selection (proxy variables), and model design (objective functions optimising accuracy over fairness).",bullets:["Historical bias in data","Proxy features (postcode → ethnicity)","Objective functions ignoring fairness"]},{heading:"Fairness Metrics",body:"Demographic parity requires equal positive rates. Equalised odds requires equal TPR and FPR. No single metric is universally appropriate.",bullets:["Demographic parity: equal positive rates","Equalised odds: equal TPR and FPR","Individual fairness: similar people → similar outcomes"]},{heading:"Governance",body:"The EU AI Act classifies systems by risk. Model cards document use and limitations. Human oversight is mandatory for high-stakes AI.",bullets:["EU AI Act: risk-based regulation","Model cards: documented responsible use","Human oversight for high-stakes AI"]}],tip:"When deploying AI, ask: who could be harmed, how would they know, what recourse exists?"}
