  notes:{c1:{title:"Variables & Types",intro:"JavaScript offers three declaration keywords: var, let, and const. Understanding type differences and coercion prevents subtle bugs.",sections:[{heading:"Declaration Keywords",body:"var is function-scoped and hoisted. let is block-scoped and modern. const is also block-scoped but cannot be reassigned.",bullets:["var: function-scoped, avoid in new code","let: block-scoped, reassignable","const: block-scoped, not reassignable","Use const by default, let when reassignment needed"]},{heading:"Primitive Types",body:"Seven primitives: string, number, bigint, boolean, undefined, symbol, null. Everything else is an object in JavaScript.",bullets:["string: 'hello', \"world\"","number: 42, 3.14 (no int/float distinction)","boolean: true, false (lowercase)","null vs undefined: set vs never assigned"]},{heading:"Type Coercion",body:"JavaScript implicitly converts types in comparisons and operations. == coerces types; === does not. Always prefer === for safety.",bullets:['"5" == 5 returns true (coerces types)','"5" === 5 returns false (strict check)','typeof x returns the type as a string','null has typeof "object" (a known JS bug)']}],tip:"Always use === for comparisons to avoid type coercion surprises."},
  c2:{title:"Functions & Arrays",intro:"Functions are first-class values in JS - they can be stored, passed, and returned. Arrays provide powerful built-in methods for data transformation.",sections:[{heading:"Function Types",body:"Function declarations are hoisted to the top of their scope. Expressions are not. Arrow functions are concise but lack their own 'this' binding.",bullets:["Declaration: function f() {}","Expression: const f = function() {}","Arrow: const f = () => {}","Arrow functions do not bind their own 'this'"]},{heading:"Array Methods",body:"map, filter, reduce transform arrays functionally. forEach iterates for side effects. find and includes search for values.",bullets:["map: transform each element into new value","filter: keep elements matching a test","reduce: accumulate into a single value","find: first element matching a test"]},{heading:"Closures",body:"A closure is a function that remembers variables from its enclosing scope, even after that scope has finished executing.",bullets:["Inner function retains access to outer variables","Used for data privacy and factory functions","Found commonly in callbacks and event handlers","Can cause memory leaks if references are kept too long"]}],tip:"Favour arrow functions for callbacks; use declarations for named reusable functions."},
  c3:{title:"DOM & Events",intro:"The Document Object Model represents HTML as a tree of objects. JavaScript manipulates the DOM to create dynamic, interactive web pages.",sections:[{heading:"DOM Tree Structure",body:"The browser parses HTML into a tree of nodes. Each element is a node. document is the root. Parents contain children; siblings share a parent.",bullets:["document is the root node of the page","Elements are nested parent-child","Text nodes hold visible content","Attribute nodes hold element properties"]},{heading:"Selecting Elements",body:"querySelector finds the first element matching a CSS selector. querySelectorAll returns all matches. getElementById is fastest for IDs.",bullets:["querySelector('.class') uses CSS selector","querySelectorAll returns a NodeList","getElementById is fastest for unique IDs","All return Element or NodeList objects"]},{heading:"Event Handling",body:"addEventListener attaches handler functions to elements. Events bubble up from target to document. event.target identifies the origin.",bullets:["el.addEventListener('click', handler)","Event object is passed to the handler function","e.target is the element that triggered it","e.preventDefault() stops default browser behaviour"]}],tip:"Use event delegation on parent elements for dynamically created content."}},
