  notes:{c1:{title:"Ownership & Borrowing",intro:"Rust's ownership system guarantees memory safety without garbage collection. Every value has exactly one owner; ownership can be moved or borrowed.",sections:[{heading:"Ownership Rules",body:"Three fundamental rules: each value has one owner, when the owner goes out of scope the value is dropped, and ownership can be moved to another variable.",bullets:["Each value has exactly one owner at a time","Owner goes out of scope and value is dropped","Assignment transfers ownership (move semantics)","Clone creates an explicit deep copy"]},{heading:"References & Borrowing",body:"References (&T) let you access data without taking ownership. Mutable references (&mut T) allow changes but are strictly exclusive.",bullets:["&T is an immutable borrow, many allowed simultaneously","&mut T is a mutable borrow, only one at a time","Cannot have &T and &mut T for same data at once","References must always be valid (no dangling pointers)"]},{heading:"Lifetimes",body:"Lifetimes ensure references never outlive the data they point to. The compiler infers most lifetimes; some require explicit annotation.",bullets:["'a annotation marks a reference lifetime","fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str","'static means the reference lives for the entire program","Prevents dangling references at compile time"]}],tip:'Think of ownership as "who is responsible for cleanup" - the compiler enforces it strictly.'},
  c2:{title:"Traits, Enums & Pattern Matching",intro:"Traits define shared behaviour across types. Enums define types by enumerating variants. Pattern matching ties them together for safe branching.",sections:[{heading:"Trait Definitions",body:"A trait defines a set of methods that types can implement. Similar to interfaces in other languages but with more flexibility.",bullets:["trait Drawable { fn draw(&self); }","impl Drawable for Circle { ... }","Trait objects: Box<dyn Trait> for dynamic dispatch","Multiple traits per type with separate impl blocks"]},{heading:"Enums & Variants",body:"Enums list all possible variants. Each variant can hold different data. Rust enums are powerful algebraic data types.",bullets:["enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }","enum Shape { Circle(f64), Rect(f64, f64) }","Variants can carry different data types","The compiler tracks which variant is active"]},{heading:"Pattern Matching",body:"match expressions destructure values and branch on patterns. The compiler ensures every case is handled exhaustively.",bullets:["match x { 1 => 'one', _ => 'other' }","Destructure tuples: (a, b) => a + b","Guard conditions: x if x > 5 => ...","if let for single-pattern matching"]}],tip:'Use match to handle every case - the compiler enforces exhaustiveness, preventing forgotten branches.'}},
